// ============================================================
// CHAPTER ONE — Welcome Home
// Saturday, August 31, 1991
// ============================================================
=== ch1 ===
@chapter Chapter One :: Welcome Home :: Saturday, August 31, 1991 — the road into Derry
@bg road rain - slow
@fx clear
@weather rain
@amb rain_car
@ctx Gus (14) rides shotgun in her mom's wood-paneled station wagon in the rain, moving from Portland into her grandmother's house in Derry after her parents' divorce. Mom is trying to make conversation.
The rain starts at the county line, like it's been waiting up for you.
Pine trees. Pine trees. A gas station with one pump and a hand-lettered sign that says NIGHTCRAWLERS. More pine trees.
Your recorder is on your knee with the mic pointed at the windshield. Rain on a car roof is one of the best sounds in the world, and you have forty minutes of it from three different cars. This is the first rain in the station wagon, though. The station wagon has a rattle.
@show mom neutral left
MOM(smile): You're taping the rain again.
* ["It's different rain."  #joke]
    MOM(laugh): It's Maine rain, honey. It's been the same rain since 1820.
    MOM(smile): But okay. Document it for the museum.
* ["The wagon has a rattle. I'm collecting evidence for the lawsuit." #sarcastic]
    MOM(smirk): You'll lose. The wagon has excellent lawyers. The wagon is *very* well connected.
    She thumps the dashboard with the heel of her hand. The rattle stops, considers, starts again.
* [(Shrug. Keep recording.) #silent]
    You shrug. The little reels keep turning.
    MOM(sad): Okay. Cool. Love that for us.
* ["Somebody should remember what this sounds like." #honest]
    MOM(surprised): Remember what? The rain?
    GUS: This. The drive. The last part before it's different.
    Your mother doesn't say anything for a while. The wipers clear their throat. Clear their throat.
    MOM(sad): Yeah. Okay. That's fair.
    ~ mom += 1
- @expr mom neutral
Your mother drives the way she used to smoke: like she isn't supposed to be doing it and wants to get it over with. She's quit the cigarettes. Now she chews Nicorette like it owes her money.
Every few minutes her eyes go to the rearview mirror. Not the road behind you — the back seat. The empty back seat, full of boxes and a laundry basket of shoes.
MOM(worried): I know this isn't what you wanted.
* ["What I wanted was Dad not marrying a woman named Trish." #blunt]
    MOM(flat): Mm. Well. That ship has sailed. And docked. And is apparently expecting in February.
    MOM(sad): I'm sorry, Gussie. I didn't mean that to come out mean. About her, I meant it. Not about you.
    ~ mom += 0
* ["It's fine." #deflect]
    MOM(smirk): "It's fine." The two most dangerous words in the English language.
    MOM(sad): You can be mad at me. I can take it. I'm extremely durable. Ask my lower back.
* ["It's not your fault. I know that." #kind]
    MOM(surprised): ...
    MOM(sad): That's — thank you. I'm not sure it's true, but thank you.
    She reaches over without looking and squeezes your knee, once, the way she used to at stoplights when you were little.
    ~ mom += 2
    @remember mom You told her it wasn't her fault.
* ["Then why are we doing it?" #ask]
    MOM(sad): Because your grandmother can't live alone anymore, and the house is paid for, and the hospital needed a night nurse, and I am forty-four years old and I have eleven hundred dollars.
    MOM(flat): That's the honest answer. You want the inspiring one, I can make one up.
    ~ mom += 1
- @ctx Mom is warning Gus about her grandmother Mémère's dementia while they drive into Derry.
MOM(neutral): Listen. About Mémère.
MOM(worried): She has good days and bad days. On bad days she might call you something else. She might not know who you are at all. Don't take it personally. It's not her. It's the disease.
* ["What does she call people?" #ask]
    MOM(smirk): Anything. Last spring she called the mailman Father Leclerc for a month. He started answering to it.
* (sister) ["Does she know who you are?" #probe]
    MOM(flat): Most of the time. She calls me by my sister's name sometimes. Which is a neat trick, since I don't have a sister.
    MOM(smile): She'll know you, though. She always asks about you. "Comment va la petite?" Every phone call.
* ["I'll be nice to her." #kind]
    MOM(smile): I know you will. You were always good with her. She used to let you win at cards, and you'd yell at her for letting you win.
    ~ mom += 1
- @expr mom neutral
* [Ask why she keeps checking the back seat. #probe]
    GUS: Why do you keep looking in the back?
    MOM(surprised): Hm?
    GUS: The mirror. You keep checking the back seat. There's nothing back there but shoes.
    MOM(neutral): Oh. Habit, I guess. You used to sit back there.
    GUS: I've sat up front since I was eleven.
    MOM(smirk): Then I'm a creature of habit. Sue me. You'll have to get in line behind the wagon.
    But she doesn't look in the mirror again for a long time, and when she does, she catches herself, and her mouth goes tight.
    ~ clue(rearview)
* [Let it go. Watch the trees. #silent]
    You don't say anything. Pine trees. The road unspools ahead of the headlights.
    In the mirror, the empty back seat. Your mother's eyes, checking it. Checking it.
    ~ clue(rearview)
- @bg road rain sign slow
@sfx static_burst 0.4
The headlights find a sign at the edge of the road.
Weathered wood, letters carved and painted white: DERRY. Below it, smaller: Welcome Home.
Below *that*, in black spray paint, somebody has written LEAVE WHILE YOU CAN. Somebody else has crossed it out and written underneath, in red: YOU CAN'T.
The needle on your recorder's level meter jumps all the way into the red, then drops. In your headphones, for one second, the rain turns into a long hiss like a breath let out.
@ctx Driving past the Derry welcome sign. Graffiti on it says LEAVE WHILE YOU CAN, crossed out, with YOU CAN'T underneath. Mom grew up here.
MOM(smirk): Kids. When I was in high school somebody painted DERRY: WE'RE NOT DEAD YET on that sign. My mother wrote a letter to the paper.
* ["Did you go to Derry High?" #ask]
    MOM(smile): Class of sixty-five. Go Tigers.
    MOM(thinking): God. I don't remember half of it, honestly. Isn't that awful? I remember the smell of the gym. I remember a boy named Ricky Shaw who could burp the national anthem.
    MOM(flat): Everything else is just... fog. Derry's like that. Ask anybody who grew up here. They'll tell you they had a nice childhood, and then they can't tell you the name of their third-grade teacher.
* ["Was it a nice place to grow up?" #friendly]
    MOM(thinking): Sure. It was nice. Nice people. Pretty in the fall. Good pie.
    MOM(flat): That's what you say, isn't it. "It was nice." That's what everybody from here says.
    She chews her gum hard.
* ["Your recorder thing just went crazy." #look]
    GUS: My meter just pinned. Right when we passed the sign.
    MOM(neutral): That's the power lines. Or a ham radio. There's a hundred guys in this county with antennas in their backyards talking to Belgium.
    MOM(smile): Welcome to Derry. Even the static's friendly.
- The rain quits the moment you turn onto Larch Street, as if it's done what it came to do.
@weather none
@amb stop
@hide mom
-> ch1_arrive

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_arrive ===
@bg larch_ext dusk
@amb night
@music larch
@ctx Gus and her mom arrive at Mémère's house, 9 Larch Street, at dusk. An eight-year-old neighbor girl, Hannah, is watching from the next porch.
Nine Larch Street is narrow and tall and green, the green of a house that was painted once with great optimism and never again. A porch with a glider. A lilac gone wild. A round window up in the peak like an eye that's been open too long.
You were born in this town. You lived here until you were six. You should remember this house better than you do.
You remember the smell. That's all. Wet wood and lilac and something sweet, like Vicks VapoRub and burnt sugar.
On the porch next door, a little girl in a pink sweatshirt is sitting on the top step with her knees under her chin, watching you unload boxes. She doesn't wave. She doesn't do anything. She just watches.
@show hannah flat right
~ met_hannah = true
HANNAH: Are you the new ones?
* ["We're the new ones. I'm Gus." #friendly]
    HANNAH(thinking): That's a boy's name.
    GUS: It's short for Augusta.
    HANNAH(flat): That's a city.
* ["Depends. Who were the old ones?" #joke]
    HANNAH(thinking): The Lavoies. They moved to Florida.
    HANNAH(flat): Florida is where old people go to be warm before they die.
* ["Shouldn't you be inside? It's getting dark." #blunt]
    HANNAH(flat): It's not October.
    She says it like that settles it.
* [(Wave, and keep carrying boxes.) #shy]
    You lift a hand. Hannah watches the hand go up and come down, like she's grading it.
- HANNAH(neutral): Are you staying for Quiet Night?
* ["What's Quiet Night?" #ask]
    HANNAH(surprised): ...
    She looks at you like you asked her what Christmas is.
    HANNAH(neutral): It's when—
* ["Sure. Sounds restful." #joke]
    HANNAH(flat): It's not restful. It's—
* [(Say nothing.) #silent]
    You don't answer. Hannah leans forward.
    HANNAH(neutral): You have to be inside by eight. Everybody is. Even the—
- A screen door bangs open behind her.
MRSGAGNON: Hannah Marie! Inside. Now.
HANNAH(flat): ...
@hide hannah
She goes without a word. A moment later the porch light next door clicks off, though it isn't dark yet, and the curtains in the front window draw shut.
~ clue(hannah_quiet)
MOM: Gus! Grab the lamp box, would you? The one that says FRAGILE in letters a foot high? Don't take the hint, take the box.
-> ch1_memere

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_memere ===
@bg living evening
@amb house
@ctx Inside Mémère's living room. Gus meets her grandmother Mémère (Yvette, 72, dementia) for the first time since she was six. Mémère is in her armchair by the window.
A woman from the visiting nurse service hands your mother a three-ring binder of medications, a look of deep sympathy, and the house.
The living room is exactly as you didn't know you remembered it. Plastic slipcovers on the flowered couch. A cuckoo clock that has stopped at 4:10. Hummel figurines in a glass cabinet, a whole tiny village of rosy-cheeked children staring out at you.
Your grandmother is in her armchair, facing the window. The TV is on with the sound off: *Wheel of Fortune*, the letters turning over with no one to see them.
@show memere neutral center
She turns. Her whole face changes.
MEMERE(smile): Junie! Oh — ma belle. Tu es revenue. You came back.
Your mother stops in the doorway like she's walked into a cobweb.
@show mom worried left
MOM: Ma. It's Augusta. Gus. Your granddaughter.
MEMERE(flat): I know who she is, Claire. I'm not senile.
MEMERE(smile): Viens ici. Come here. Let me look at you.
~ clue(junie)
@ctx Mémère has just called Gus "Junie" and asked her to come closer. Her cardigan is buttoned wrong.
Her cardigan is mauve and buttoned wrong, so one side hangs an inch lower than the other. There's a little brass pin on the collar, shaped like a lantern.
* [(Go to her. Let her look at you.) #kind]
    You cross the room and crouch by the chair. She takes your face in both hands. Her fingers are cold and smell like Jergens lotion.
    MEMERE(smile): You got tall. And you got the Thibodeau chin. Pauvre petite.
    MEMERE(laugh): Don't worry, it suits a girl better. On your Uncle Marcel it was a tragedy.
    ~ mem += 1
* [(Fix her buttons.) #kind]
    You kneel beside her chair and undo the buttons and do them up again, one by one, matching them. She holds very still and watches your hands like they're doing a magic trick.
    MEMERE(sad): Junie always did that.
    MEMERE(smile): ...There. Now I look like somebody.
    ~ mem += 2
    @remember memere You fixed her buttons.
* (asked_junie) ["Hi, Mémère. Who's Junie?" #ask]
    MEMERE(thinking): ...
    MOM(neutral): She used to call me Junebug. When I was little. She gets her decades mixed up.
    MEMERE(flat): I don't get anything mixed up. I get things *put away.* That's different.
    MOM(smirk): Okay, Ma.
    {ch1.sister:
        THINK: In the car, Mom said Mémère calls her by her sister's name — the sister she doesn't have. Now it's a pet name from when she was little. She doesn't seem to notice she's told it two different ways.
        ~ clue(mom_patch)
    }
* ["Nice sweater." #friendly]
    MEMERE(smile): Sears, 1974. Things lasted, then. Now everything is made in a basement in Taiwan by a man who hates you.
    MOM: Ma.
    MEMERE(smirk): What? He does. I've seen the stitching.
* [(Hang back by the door.) #shy]
    You stay where you are. Mémère squints at you across the room.
    MEMERE(neutral): She's shy. She was always shy. Claire, she was always so shy.
    MOM(flat): Ma, you haven't seen her since she was six.
    MEMERE(flat): I know when I've seen people.
- @expr memere neutral
MEMERE(neutral): You'll go to the high school. Wednesday.
GUS: Yeah.
MEMERE(worried): And you'll come straight home. In October. Everybody comes straight home in October.
MOM(smile): Okay! Who's hungry? I'm thinking tourtière. Ma, there's tourtière in the freezer from Christmas?
MEMERE(laugh): Which Christmas?
@hide all
-> ch1_dinner

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_dinner ===
@bg kitchen evening
@ctx Dinner in Mémère's kitchen on the first night: microwaved tourtière (meat pie). Mom absent-mindedly sets an extra plate.
The kitchen has yellow wallpaper with tiny daisies, a wall phone with a cord long enough to reach the bathroom, and a crucifix over the window watching the sink. The tourtière comes out of the microwave steaming on the outside and frozen solid in the middle, which Mémère says is how her mother made it too.
Your mother sets the table while she talks about the hospital, the night shifts, the parking.
One plate. Two plates. Three plates.
Four.
@show mom neutral left
@show memere neutral right
* ["Expecting somebody?" #probe]
    MOM(neutral): Hm?
    GUS: Four plates.
    Your mother looks at the fourth plate like it's a word she can't remember how to spell.
    MOM(flat): Huh. Math. I'm bad at math. I worked eleven nights in a row in June, I think something got shaken loose.
    She puts the plate back in the cupboard. Her hand shakes a little when she does it.
    ~ asked_four_plates = true
* [(Quietly put the extra plate back yourself.) #kind]
    You pick up the fourth plate and slide it back into the cupboard. Your mother doesn't notice. She's still talking about the parking garage.
    Your grandmother notices. She watches you put it away, and her lips move, like she's counting under her breath.
    ~ mem += 1
* [(Say nothing.) #silent]
    You let it sit there. Nobody sits in front of it. Nobody puts anything on it. It stays there through the whole meal, white and empty, like a window.
- ~ clue(four_plates)
@expr mom smile
MOM(smile): So. Derry High. You're going to love it. Or hate it. Either way, it's character-building.
MEMERE(smile): The Ashgrove children will be there. Constance's twins. Such *nice* children.
MOM(smirk): Constance Ashgrove still runs everything?
MEMERE(neutral): Somebody has to.
MEMERE(smile): Augusta. Your father. Is he still a nice man with no chin?
* ["He's still nice. Chin's still missing." #joke]
    MEMERE(laugh): Ha! Bon. Some things you can count on.
    MOM(laugh): Ma!
    ~ mem += 1
* ["He got remarried." #blunt]
    MEMERE(flat): Mm. To the girl with the hair.
    MOM(flat): Her name is Trish.
    MEMERE(flat): That's what I said.
* ["Mémère. Be nice." #friendly]
    MEMERE(smirk): I'm seventy-two years old. I'm done being nice. I was nice from 1919 to 1990. Look where it got me. Larch Street.
- @expr memere neutral
For a while there's only the sound of forks, and the clock, and the refrigerator humming to itself.
MEMERE(thinking): The Lantern Float is in October. On the canal. You'll see. It's the most beautiful thing you ever saw. All the little lights on the water.
MEMERE(sad): For the ones we lost.
GUS: What ones?
MEMERE(neutral): ...
MEMERE(flat): Hm? What ones what?
MOM(neutral): Okay. Ma, pills. The yellow one and the two white ones. Gus, can you start on dishes?
@hide all
-> ch1_room

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_room ===
@bg gus_room night
@amb night
@music stop 4
@ctx Gus alone in her new bedroom at 9 Larch Street on the first night, unpacking. The room has daisy wallpaper, a closet with old pencil height marks on the doorframe, moving boxes, and a window over the street.
Your room is the back bedroom, with a slanted ceiling you'll hit your head on at least twice a week. Yellow daisy wallpaper, faded almost to cream. An iron bed with a quilt that smells like cedar.
Mom says it was the sewing room. It doesn't look like a sewing room. It looks like somebody's room.
- (room)
* [Look out the window. #look]
    Larch Street. The streetlight on the corner buzzes and blinks, deciding whether to stay on. Every house has its porch light on except the Gagnons'.
    Across the street, in an upstairs window, a curtain moves.
    Probably a cat. Derry seems like a cat town.
    -> room
* [Check the closet. #look]
    The closet door sticks. Inside: empty hangers, a smell of mothballs, a box of Christmas ornaments wrapped in the Derry News from 1979.
    Somebody marked heights on the inside of the doorframe in pencil. Little ticks climbing up the wood, most of them painted over at least once.
    ~ saw_heights = true
    * * [Look closer at the marks. #look]
        You crouch. The low ones say C and a year: C '51. C '53. C '55. Your mother, shorter than a doorknob.
        Higher up, under a layer of paint, are other ticks with a different letter. You can't make it out. J? I? The years are gone.
        And at the very bottom, barely two feet off the floor, one tick that isn't painted over at all. Neat block letters: G — 1981.
        You were four in 1981. You don't remember ever being measured here.
        ~ clue(height_marks)
    * * [Leave it.]
        You close the closet. Old houses have old pencil marks. That's all they are.
    - - -> room
* [Unpack your tapes. #action]
    The box says GUS — TAPES — DO NOT DROP (THIS MEANS YOU, MOM). Forty-one cassettes in a shoebox, each one labeled in your tiny capitals.
    RAIN \#3 (DAD'S CAR). PORTLAND FERRY HORN. KITCHEN AT 2 A.M. THE TRAIN AT YARMOUTH. MRS. BELLAMY'S WIND CHIMES. A SEAGULL THAT SOUNDS LIKE A BABY.
    Other kids collect stamps. You collect the sound of places, so that when you leave them you can still go back.
    You've reused most of these tapes so many times the labels are three layers deep. You buy blanks when you can. Mostly you can't.
    -> room
* [Look at the photo of Dad. #look]
    Dad at the mixing board at the studio in Boston, headphones around his neck, giving the camera a thumbs-up. He sent it in a card for your birthday with a twenty-dollar bill and *Love you kiddo, more soon!!*
    It's been "more soon" since February.
    -> room
+ [Go to bed. #action]
    -> ch1_dad

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_dad ===
@bg kitchen night
@amb house
@ctx Gus's dad Danny calls from Boston to check that they arrived. Gus talks to him on the kitchen wall phone. The divorce is recent; he has remarried (Trish) and they're expecting a baby.
@sfx phone_ring
Before you can get into bed, the phone rings downstairs. Your mother calls up that it's for you, in the voice she uses for your father, which is her regular voice with all the furniture taken out.
You sit on the kitchen floor with your back against the cabinets and the cord stretched across the linoleum.
PHONE(dad): Hey, kiddo. You guys make it okay?
* ["We made it. The wagon has a rattle." #friendly]
    PHONE(dad): The wagon's had a rattle since 1986. It's load-bearing. Don't let anybody fix it.
* ["We're here." #blunt]
    PHONE(dad): Okay. Good. Good, good.
    A pause long enough to drive a truck through.
* ["Trish let you call?" #sarcastic]
    PHONE(dad): ...Gus.
    PHONE(dad): That's not — come on. That's not fair.
    ~ dad -= 1
- PHONE(dad): How's your grandmother?
GUS: She called me Junie.
PHONE(dad): ...
PHONE(dad): Huh.
There's a strange little silence on the line, like the sound of someone standing at the top of stairs in the dark.
PHONE(dad): Well. She's — you know. She's confused. Hey, you still got that recorder I gave you?
GUS: It's surgically attached.
PHONE(dad): I gave you that? Huh.
GUS: For my seventh birthday. The red one. You don't remember?
PHONE(dad): No, right, yeah. Sure. God, I'm getting old. Trish says I can't remember where I put my keys if they're in my hand.
You look at the recorder on your knee. Red, scuffed silver at the corners, the brand name worn off where your thumb goes. You've had it as long as you can remember. You have always known he gave it to you.
~ clue(dad_recorder)
PHONE(dad): Listen, kiddo, I gotta go. Trish's got this — there's a class thing. Breathing. You breathe in a room with other people. Seventy bucks.
* ["I love you, Dad." #kind]
    PHONE(dad): Love you too, Gussie. More than — yeah. A lot. Call me after your first day?
    ~ dad += 1
    ~ dad_call_warm = true
* ["You could have fought for us, you know." #honest]
    PHONE(dad): ...
    PHONE(dad): I know. I know I could've. I'm — Gus, I'm sorry. I'm not good at — I'm sorry.
    He sounds like he means it. That's the worst part. He always means it.
    ~ dad += 0
* ["Have fun breathing." #sarcastic]
    PHONE(dad): Ha. Yeah. I'll breathe one for you.
* [(Hang up.) #leave]
    You hold the phone away from your ear and look at it while it says your name. Then you put it in the cradle, very gently, like it's asleep.
    ~ dad -= 1
- @sfx phone_hangup
-> ch1_night

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_night ===
@bg living night
@amb house
@music june
@ctx 2 a.m. on the first night. Gus finds Mémère standing at the dark living room window, singing an old French-Canadian song, "À la claire fontaine."
You can't sleep. The house makes noises you don't know yet: the radiator ticks, the stairs settle, something in the walls goes *tuk... tuk...* like a slow finger on a table.
At 2:10 by your Timex, you hear singing.
You go down the stairs in your socks, keeping to the edges where the treads don't creak, a trick you didn't know you knew.
@show memere sad center
Your grandmother is standing at the front window in her nightgown with the curtains open, looking out at Larch Street. The streetlight paints a long stripe across her. She's singing, very softly, in French.
MEMERE(sad): *À la claire fontaine, m'en allant promener...*
MEMERE(sad): *J'ai trouvé l'eau si belle, que je m'y suis baignée...*
And you know it.
You don't know how you know it. The tune comes up out of you like something you swallowed years ago. You know where it goes next, the little climb and fall. You've hummed it on buses. In waiting rooms. Under your breath when you're scared. You always thought you made it up.
~ clue(claire_song)
MEMERE(sad): *Il y a longtemps que je t'aime... jamais je ne t'oublierai.*
* [Ask what the song means. #ask]
    GUS: Mémère? What does it mean?
    MEMERE(surprised): Oh! Mon Dieu. You scared me.
    MEMERE(sad): It means... "I've loved you for a long time. I will never forget you."
    She laughs, very quietly, at something you can't see.
    MEMERE(flat): It's a lie, the song. Everybody forgets. That's the only thing in this town you can count on.
* [Hum along with her. #kind]
    You hum the last line with her. Your voice is rough with sleep.
    She stops singing. She turns around and stares at you, and in the stripe of streetlight her eyes are wet and very clear.
    MEMERE(afraid): Who taught you that?
    GUS: I don't know. I just... know it.
    MEMERE(sad): Junie. Junie taught you that.
    ~ mem += 1
    @remember memere You knew her song.
* [Take her arm and walk her back to bed. #kind]
    GUS: Come on, Mémère. It's late.
    She lets you take her arm. She weighs almost nothing. On the way down the hall, she stops, and grips your wrist hard enough to hurt.
    MEMERE(worried): Don't you open that door after dark. In October. Not for anybody. You hear me?
    GUS: ...Okay.
    MEMERE(flat): Not for *anybody.*
    ~ mem += 1
* [Record her singing. #record]
    You thumb the red button. The little reels turn. She doesn't notice, or doesn't care. She sings the whole song through again for the window, for the street, for whoever she's singing it to.
    ~ tape(tape_memere)
    When she finishes she says, to the glass, "Bonne nuit, ma belle," and goes back to bed without seeing you at all.
- @hide memere
-> ch1_tape

// ------------------------------------------------------------
=== ch1_tape ===
@bg gus_room night
@music stop 3
@amb night
@ctx Back in bed at night, Gus plays back the rain recording from the car. Something is on it.
Back in bed. Headphones on. You rewind the car tape to the beginning and let it play, because sometimes the only way to fall asleep somewhere new is to listen to somewhere else.
The rain. The wipers. Your mother saying *You're taping the rain again*, tinny and far away.
The counter rolls toward the place where you passed the sign. The hiss rises like a wave.
@sfx tape_play
@wait 1
And under the hiss, a voice.
A girl's voice. Not yours. Older than you, a little. Close to the microphone, the way you'd talk to someone under the covers with a flashlight.
TAPE(girl): —it means you came back—
Then just rain.
* [Rewind. Listen again. #record]
    @sfx tape_rewind
    @wait 1.2
    You rewind it, hands not quite steady, and play it again.
    TAPE(girl): —if you're hearing this, Gussie, it means you came back—
    \*Gussie.* Nobody calls you that except your parents. And Mémère. And — nobody.
    You play it again. And again. The voice is the same every time, which is somehow worse than if it changed.
    ~ heard_sign_voice = true
    ~ clue(sign_voice)
    ~ tape(tape_car)
    * * ["It's crosstalk. A radio station bleeding through." #honest]
        THINK: Radio. That's all. Mom said it: a hundred guys in this county talking to Belgium. Some kid's radio drama, bleeding onto the tape.
        THINK(flat): A kid's radio drama that knows your nickname.
        @theory voice_radio
    * * ["I've reused this tape a hundred times. It's something old, coming through." #probe]
        THINK: These tapes are ancient. Some of them came out of a box at a yard sale. When you record over a tape, sometimes the old recording doesn't totally go away. Print-through. Ghosting. Dad told you that once.
        THINK(worried): But you don't remember ever taping a girl saying your name.
        @theory voice_old_tape
    * * ["That's not possible." #shy]
        THINK(afraid): It isn't possible. It isn't. You turn the tape over in your hands like it might explain itself.
        @theory voice_impossible
* [Record over it. Right now. #action]
